      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--blue);background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:9px 13px;margin-bottom:14px;line-height:1.7">
        <strong style="color:var(--text)">Print from a desktop or laptop browser.</strong> Mobile browsers do not support the precision printing this guide requires. Open this file in Chrome or Firefox on a computer, then use the print button above. Do not scale, shrink-to-fit, or fit-to-page — print at exactly 100%.
      </div>

      <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--amber);background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:8px 12px;margin-bottom:16px;line-height:1.7">
        <strong>Note:</strong> The 2mg and 8mg films are physically identical in size (22×12.8mm). They differ only in drug concentration — the 8mg film contains 3.2× more buprenorphine per square millimeter. A 1/8 cut of a 2mg film = 250mcg. A 1/8 cut of an 8mg film = 1000mcg. Always verify the imprint before cutting.
      </div>

      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text2);line-height:1.9;padding:14px;background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius)">
        <div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--text3);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:10px">how to cut accurately</div>

        <p style="margin:0 0 9px">Select the film strength above, hit <strong style="color:var(--text)">print</strong>, and print at exactly 100% scale — no fit-to-page, no shrink-to-fit. The printed guide will be the exact physical size of the film. Verify with a ruler: the strip should measure exactly 22mm wide before you touch it with a blade.</p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 9px"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Use a sharp hobby knife with a fresh blade.</strong> A dull blade drags and tears the film instead of cutting it. One new blade costs less than a wasted strip. X-Acto #11 or a snap-off utility blade both work. Replace the blade if you feel any resistance.</p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 9px"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Cut on glass.</strong> A smooth glass surface — a picture frame insert, a glass cutting board — gives you a flat, hard backing with no give. Cutting on cardboard or a soft mat lets the blade wander. The film won't slip on glass, which matters at this scale.</p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 9px"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Put your eyes where the blade needs to go, not where it is.</strong> Look ahead of the cut along your guide line. Your hand follows your eyes. Looking at the blade itself is what produces drift.</p>

        <p style="margin:0 0 9px"><strong style="color:var(--text)">Several light quick strokes, not one heavy push.</strong> Score the film with two or three passes of minimal pressure before committing to a full cut. Light passes stay on line. A single heavy stroke wants to skate off it. Once you feel the blade reach the glass, one clean final pass finishes it.</p>

        <p style="margin:0">Place the printed guide under the film if the film is translucent enough, or lay it alongside as a measuring reference and use a metal straight edge — never a plastic ruler — aligned to the grid lines. The drug is uniformly distributed throughout the film matrix, so a geometrically accurate cut is a dosimetrically accurate cut.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:10px 14px;margin-bottom:12px;font-size:11px;color:var(--text2);line-height:1.8">
      <div style="margin-bottom:4px;font-size:10px;color:var(--text3);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.1em">on film vs tablet</div>
      Films are the better choice for precision tapering. The drug is distributed uniformly throughout the film matrix, so a geometrically accurate cut produces a proportionally accurate dose. Tablets cannot be cut accurately — their active ingredient is not uniformly distributed, and the cut surface area introduces variability. This app is designed around films.
    </div>

