      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">data</span> &nbsp;Clearing browser data, changing browsers, or changing devices may erase your records unless you create a backup.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">how the occupancy estimate works</span> &nbsp;This app estimates <b>μ-opioid receptor occupancy</b>, not milligrams, because occupancy tracks the biology. It chains a dose-log-derived on-board level (one-compartment model, half-life 36 hr, time-to-peak 2 hr) through estimated plasma concentration to a saturable Emax curve calibrated to PET [¹¹C]carfentanil data (Greenwald 2003). Above ~4 mg the curve saturates and occupancy barely moves; the meaningful range is low-dose.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">the steep zone &amp; reference lines</span> &nbsp;The shaded band is where small dose changes move occupancy the most. The ~50%/~70% dashed lines are clinical targets from an <b>active-use context</b>, shown for orientation only — a stable low-dose taperer functions well below them.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">where tapers end — don't chase zero</span> &nbsp;A taper has a real endpoint. The <b>2 mg/day dose (~40% occupancy) is the recognized hardest step</b>; below it, decrements are typically 0.25–0.5 mg every couple of weeks. Most people <b>discontinue from around 0.25–0.5 mg/day (~8–15% occupancy)</b>, often with clinician support. The goal is reaching that zone and stopping, not driving the percentage to zero. Plan the final jump with your prescriber.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">variability &amp; limits</span> &nbsp;Absorption varies dose-to-dose. The Modifier toggle shows a ±1 SD absorption band (within-person CV ≈ 34%). This is a population-average estimate, not a lab measurement — trajectory and threshold-crossings are trustworthy, but precision drops at lower doses.</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">disclaimer</span> &nbsp;This tool is for informational and self-tracking purposes only and is not medical advice. Your decisions are your own, to be made with a licensed physician.</div>
